Limitations of My Model
I built this model as my first try at predicting heart disease, and it got about 84% accuracy, which is cool, but there are some issues I spotted that might make it less reliable.

Lots of Missing Data: Stuff like the number of major vessels (ca) is missing in over half the rows, and things like thal (some heart test) are gone in about 50%. I filled them in with averages or common values, but that might mess up the predictions if the guesses are off. Maybe I should try a better way to handle blanks next time, or just drop those columns if they're too spotty.
Weird or Wrong Values: There are zeros in cholesterol levels where that doesn't make sense (like, people can't have zero cholesterol), and some negative numbers in oldpeak that should probably be positive. I didn't fix those, so the model might be learning from bad data. I think I need to clean that up by turning zeros into blanks or something.
Data from Different Places: The dataset mixes info from hospitals in Cleveland, Hungary, and others, and they don't all look the same—some have more missing bits or zeros. My model might do great on one group's data but flop on another's. To fix this, I could split the training by source or something.
Biases in the Data: It's mostly guys (like 80%) and older people (average age around 53), so it might not work well for women or younger folks. Also, the disease classes aren't perfectly balanced, which could make it miss some cases.
Other Stuff: The dataset's small (under 1000 rows after cleaning), so it might overfit—meaning it memorizes the training data but sucks on new stuff. I used a simple split for testing, but cross-validation could help check that. Plus, I encoded some categories in a way that assumes order when there isn't one, which might confuse things.

Overall, these are beginner mistakes, but spotting them helps me improve. For version 2, I'll clean the data better and test more ways.
